data: {"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"**Redis** is fast and handles expiry natively, so abandoned carts can age out with a TTL and no cleanup job. The downsides are that it's usually another piece of infrastructure to run, and data loss is possible unless persistence is configured carefully. **Postgres** stores carts durably alongside orders and customer data, makes abandoned-cart queries easy, and adds no new infrastructure if you already run it. It's a little slower per request and needs a scheduled job to delete old carts. Either way, the cookie should hold only an opaque cart ID, which removes the 4 KB limit.\n\nI'd recommend **Postgres**, assuming you already run it. Carts are business data, and keeping them in the same database as orders avoids a second system to operate. Redis is the better pick only if carts are truly disposable and you don't already run Redis for something else."}}

