data: {"type":"content_block_delta","index":2,"delta":{"type":"input_json_delta","partial_json":"{\"kind\": \"decision\", \"title\": \"Where should cart data live: Redis or Postgres?\", \"body\": \"Carts currently sit in a 4 KB session cookie, so long carts get truncated. Both options fix this by storing the cart server-side and keeping only a cart ID in the cookie.\\n\\n- **Redis:** fast, native TTL expiry for abandoned carts. Adds infrastructure unless you already run it, and needs persistence configured to avoid losing carts on failure.\\n- **Postgres:** durable, shares a database with orders, easy abandoned-cart queries. No new infrastructure if you already run it, but needs a cleanup job for old carts.\\n\\nRecommendation: Postgres, if it's already in your stack. Choose Redis only if carts are disposable and you already run Redis.\", \"actions\": [{\"label\":\"Use Postgres (Recommended)\",\"kind\":\"resolve\",\"recommended\":true},{\"label\":\"Use Redis\",\"kind\":\"resolve\"}]}"}}

